Large-scale supply chain optimization problems often exceed the computational capabilities of monolithic mixed-integer linear programming solvers, resulting in long solve times or failure to find feasible solutions within practical time constraints. This thesis develops and evaluates a stage-based decomposition approach that partitions supply chain networks along their natural boundaries into procurement, production and distribution subnetworks. The subnetworks are solved sequentially through a two-phase procedure: a bottom-up phase propagates demand requirements from customers toward suppliers, followed by a top-down phase that allocates actual supplies based on upstream capacity. Link nodes at partition boundaries enable information exchange between subnetworks, while aggregation heuristics propagate cost, lead time and capacity information to guide local optimization decisions.

Evaluation on 13 real customer scenarios from SAP Integrated Business Planning demonstrates the effectiveness of the approach. Across the 10 scenarios for which both approaches produced feasible solutions, decomposition achieved an average relative objective-value improvement of 23% compared to monolithic solving under identical solver-time budgets. For large-scale scenarios with high monolithic optimality gaps, improvements reach up to 92%. The approach proves particularly effective for supply chains with clear stage separation, achieving near-optimal solutions. Decomposition also enables solving scenarios that exceed monolithic solver capabilities entirely, producing feasible solutions where monolithic approaches fail to find any solution within the given time budget.
